 \begin{abstract}
-Fault assessment can conflate preserved neural transmission with task tolerance when cohorts, exposure and control laws differ. We quantify these dependencies in a 138{,}639-neuron \emph{Drosophila} spiking model. A frozen-calibration comparison crosses two laws with healthy, 25\%-deletion and 50\%-deletion networks on six paired seed clusters, comprising 72 records. Collision-free counts are 4/4/0 of 12 under the original law and 6/3/0 under pre-cue gating. Gated-minus-original net fault loss is +25.0 and +16.7 percentage points at the two doses. The 25\% conditional percentile range excludes zero, but only three clusters have nonzero contrasts; a post-hoc sign-flip sensitivity gives $p=0.25$. All factorial $+30^\circ$ episodes collide. Archive reanalysis removes 12 of 14 additional rejections relative to transmission-only scoring when targeted hubs are excluded. Duration matching changes the median deletion spike ratio from 0.382 to 0.729; six equal-duration hub pairs instead cross twofold at 840--900\,ms on calibration scenarios. These conditional findings support reporting cohorts, paired exposure, analysis horizons and task outcomes together, without establishing a resource budget or broad reliability.
+Task-level fault loss in a spiking controller can depend on feedback even when healthy success counts match. We provide an auditable paired assessment with shared input variates and deletion masks, causal feedback, exact scheduled actuation and independent input/geometry replay. A 138,639-neuron fly-connectome model is tested on 24 new seed clusters crossing two laws, two bearings and three network conditions. All 96 healthy episodes remain collision-free through one second. At 50\% connection-row deletion, proportional and relay control succeed in 37/48 and 48/48 episodes; their paired loss difference is 22.9 percentage points, with a 95\% cluster interval of 12.5--33.3 and Holm-adjusted $p=0.00195$. The 25\% contrast remains uncertain. A frozen 400-ms command-rate criterion detects only 2/13 later collisions. Geometry-informed forecasting detects all 13 but flags 57/275 successful tasks. Task loss changes with the declared horizon; stopped-duration matching leaves new group activity medians unchanged. These findings support paired task assessment without establishing biological or hardware fault tolerance.
